# TEMPLATE ONLY - production worker deployment notes; no duplicate credentials here.
# Standalone Selenium CLI needs no database, Supabase Auth or LLM provider key.
# Browser Use is kept for local experiments, not the server worker.
# The standalone CLI loads .env; this file does not auto-select a CLI environment.
#
# Integrated Compose starts the BACKEND queue worker with a Selenium subprocess.
# Select backend private configuration explicitly, e.g. from the crawler repo:
# docker compose --env-file ../backend-careeros/.env.production \
#   -f compose.production.yml up -d crawler-worker
# Adjust sibling folder names if your checkout uses careeros-backend instead.
#
# Set these in BACKEND .env.production, NOT a second crawler private env:
# DATABASE_URL=<matching environment PostgreSQL direct/session connection>
# CRAWLER_BROWSER_MODE=headless
# CRAWLER_RUN_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=3600
# CRAWLER_LEASE_SECONDS=30
# CRAWLER_MAX_CONCURRENCY=1
# CRAWLER_WORKER_REPLICAS=1
# Capacity and process count are separate: set both=2 for the two-account test.
# Native/standalone CLI does not use the worker replica variables.
# Portal policy CRAWLER_ENABLED_PORTALS belongs to the backend API.
# Per-account keywords, portals, fixed-WIB schedule and On/Off live in the database.
# Compose supplies internal Python paths and isolates artifacts/cache per project.
# No Supabase API key, Google key or Browser Use key is passed to this worker.
#
# Promote/rebuild the production-enabled backend worker source with this image.
# CURRENT BLOCKERS: backend production Compose lacks dispatcher/stats;
# crawler production Compose lacks replica and
# browser-mode env wiring. Resolve/test on dev -> staging before promoting to main.
# Updating this example does not enable those missing runtime capabilities.
